// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"Earth is like a giant layered ball. Each layer has different properties that shape our planet's surface.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Crust: the thin, rocky outer layer. Continental crust is thicker and less dense; oceanic crust is thinner and denser.","Mantle: hot, slowly flowing rock. The upper part (asthenosphere) is soft enough to flow.","Outer core: liquid iron and nickel. Its movement creates Earth's magnetic field.","Inner core: solid iron and nickel, kept solid by enormous pressure despite extreme heat."]},{"t":"key","text":"The lithosphere (crust plus the rigid upper mantle) is broken into tectonic plates that float on the softer asthenosphere."},{"t":"p","text":"Scientists learned about Earth's interior by studying seismic waves from earthquakes: S waves can't travel through liquid, which revealed the liquid outer core."}]},{"say":"Rocks are constantly being made, destroyed and remade. That never-ending process is the rock cycle.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Minerals are naturally occurring, inorganic solids with a definite chemical makeup and crystal structure. They're identified by properties like hardness (Mohs scale), luster, streak and cleavage."},{"t":"key","text":"Three rock types form in different ways:"},{"t":"list","items":["Igneous rocks form when magma or lava cools (granite, basalt).","Sedimentary rocks form when sediments are compacted and cemented (sandstone, limestone). They often contain fossils.","Metamorphic rocks form when heat and pressure change existing rock (marble from limestone, slate from shale)."]},{"t":"p","text":"In the rock cycle, any rock type can become any other through weathering, erosion, melting, heat and pressure."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Cooling magma and crystal size","aim":"Cool molten rock at different rates and measure the size of the crystals that form.","sets":[{"name":"r","start":1,"label":"Cooling rate (°C per year)","min":0.1,"max":100,"step":0.1}],"outs":[{"name":"s","expr":"5/sqrt(r)","label":"Crystal size (mm)"}],"steps":["Record crystal size for slow cooling (deep underground) and fast cooling (at the surface)."],"noise":5,"result":"Slow cooling gives crystals time to grow, so intrusive rocks like granite have large crystals. Fast-cooled extrusive rocks like basalt have tiny crystals, and instantly quenched lava forms glassy obsidian.","plot":{"x":"r","y":"s"}}]}],[{"say":"Earth's surface is broken into plates that move a few centimeters each year. Where they meet, dramatic things happen.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Plate tectonics: Earth's lithosphere is divided into plates that move on the asthenosphere, driven by mantle convection."},{"t":"list","items":["Divergent boundaries: plates pull apart, forming mid-ocean ridges and rift valleys (seafloor spreading).","Convergent boundaries: plates collide. Ocean-continent collisions form subduction zones and volcanic mountains; continent-continent collisions form huge ranges like the Himalayas.","Transform boundaries: plates slide past each other, causing earthquakes (the San Andreas Fault)."]},{"t":"p","text":"Evidence for plate tectonics includes matching fossils and rocks on different continents, the puzzle-like fit of continents, and magnetic stripes on the seafloor."}]},{"say":"Most earthquakes and volcanoes happen along plate boundaries. Understanding them helps save lives.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"An earthquake happens when stress builds along a fault and rock suddenly slips. The focus is where it starts underground; the epicenter is the point on the surface directly above."},{"t":"key","text":"Seismic waves: P waves are fastest and travel through solids and liquids; S waves are slower and only travel through solids; surface waves cause the most damage. Magnitude measures energy released."},{"t":"p","text":"Volcanoes form where magma reaches the surface, at subduction zones, divergent boundaries and hot spots (like Hawaii). Magma with lots of silica is thick and explosive; low-silica magma (basalt) flows gently."},{"t":"p","text":"The \"Ring of Fire\" around the Pacific Ocean has many earthquakes and volcanoes because of subduction zones."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Locating an earthquake","aim":"Use the delay between P-wave and S-wave arrivals on a seismogram to find the distance to the epicenter.","sets":[{"name":"lag","start":20,"label":"S minus P delay (s)","min":1,"max":120,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"d","expr":"lag*6*3.5/(6 - 3.5)","label":"Distance to epicenter (km)"}],"steps":["Record the distance for at least 4 delays.","Three stations' distances are needed to pinpoint the epicenter (triangulation)."],"noise":2,"result":"P waves (about 6 km/s) outrun S waves (about 3.5 km/s), so the delay grows steadily with distance: about 8.4 km for every second of lag. Circles from three stations meet at the epicenter.","plot":{"x":"lag","y":"d"}}]}],[{"say":"The atmosphere is a thin blanket of gases that protects life and creates our weather.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The atmosphere is mostly nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%). Its layers: troposphere (where weather happens), stratosphere (contains the ozone layer), mesosphere, thermosphere."},{"t":"key","text":"Uneven heating of Earth by the Sun drives weather. Warm air rises and cool air sinks, creating air pressure differences and wind (air moves from high to low pressure)."},{"t":"list","items":["Fronts: boundaries between air masses. Cold fronts bring short, intense storms; warm fronts bring longer, gentler rain.","Water cycle: evaporation, condensation (clouds) and precipitation.","The Coriolis effect, caused by Earth's rotation, curves winds and ocean currents."]},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Weather balloon","aim":"Launch a weather balloon and record the air pressure and temperature as it rises.","sets":[{"name":"h","start":2,"label":"Altitude (km)","min":0,"max":11,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"P","expr":"1013*exp(-h/8.4)","label":"Air pressure (hPa)"},{"name":"T","expr":"15 - 6.5*h","label":"Temperature (°C)"}],"steps":["Record pressure and temperature every 2 km up to 11 km."],"noise":1,"result":"Pressure falls exponentially with height (halving about every 5.5 km) because there's less air above. In the troposphere temperature drops about 6.5 °C per km, since air is heated from the ground.","plot":{"x":"h","y":"P"}}]},{"say":"Weather is what's happening today. Climate is the long-term pattern. And Earth's climate is changing.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Climate is the average weather in a region over many years. It depends on latitude, elevation, nearness to oceans, ocean currents and prevailing winds."},{"t":"key","text":"The greenhouse effect: gases like CO₂ and methane trap heat in the atmosphere, keeping Earth warm enough for life. Burning fossil fuels has increased CO₂, strengthening the greenhouse effect and warming the planet."},{"t":"p","text":"Evidence of climate change: rising global temperatures, melting glaciers and ice sheets, rising sea levels and more extreme weather. Solutions include renewable energy, efficiency and protecting forests."}]}],[{"say":"Earth's tilt, the Moon's orbit and the Sun's light create seasons, moon phases, eclipses and tides.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Seasons are caused by Earth's 23.5° tilt, not by distance from the Sun. When your hemisphere tilts toward the Sun, sunlight is more direct and days are longer, so it's summer."},{"t":"list","items":["Moon phases happen because we see different amounts of the Moon's sunlit half as it orbits Earth (about every 29.5 days).","A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon blocks the Sun; a lunar eclipse when Earth's shadow falls on the Moon.","Tides are caused mainly by the Moon's gravity pulling on Earth's oceans. Most places get two high and two low tides each day."]},{"t":"oops","text":"Earth is actually closest to the Sun in January, during Northern Hemisphere winter. Distance doesn't cause seasons."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Sun angle and the seasons","aim":"Measure the noon Sun's height and the energy it delivers at different latitudes and times of year.","sets":[{"name":"lat","start":40,"label":"Latitude (° N)","min":0,"max":66,"step":1},{"name":"dec","start":23.4,"label":"Sun's declination (°)","min":-23.4,"max":23.4,"step":0.1}],"outs":[{"name":"A","expr":"min(90 - lat + dec, 180 - (90 - lat + dec))","label":"Noon Sun angle (°)"},{"name":"I","expr":"max(sind(A), 0)*100","label":"Energy per m² (% of overhead Sun)"}],"steps":["Use declination +23.4° (June solstice) and record energy at 4 latitudes.","Switch to −23.4° (December) and compare."],"noise":1,"result":"The Sun is higher in summer, so its energy is concentrated on a smaller area. Seasons come from Earth's 23.4° tilt changing the Sun's angle, not from changes in distance to the Sun.","plot":{"x":"lat","y":"I"}}]},{"say":"Our Sun is one of billions of stars in our galaxy, which is one of billions of galaxies.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The solar system formed about 4.6 billion years ago from a spinning cloud of gas and dust. The four inner planets (Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars) are rocky; the four outer planets (Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune) are gas and ice giants."},{"t":"key","text":"Stars produce energy by nuclear fusion, turning hydrogen into helium. A star's life depends on its mass: Sun-like stars become white dwarfs; massive stars explode as supernovas and can leave neutron stars or black holes."},{"t":"p","text":"Our Sun is in the Milky Way galaxy. The Big Bang theory says the universe began about 13.8 billion years ago and is still expanding. Evidence includes redshift of distant galaxies and the cosmic microwave background."}]}],[{"say":"Earth provides everything we need: water, soil, minerals and energy. Using them wisely is a big challenge.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Only about 3% of Earth's water is fresh, and most of that is frozen in ice caps. Groundwater is stored in aquifers, layers of permeable rock, and can be pumped faster than it refills."},{"t":"key","text":"Renewable resources (sunlight, wind, water, trees) can be replaced naturally. Nonrenewable resources (fossil fuels, minerals) form over millions of years and can run out."},{"t":"p","text":"Soil forms slowly from weathered rock and organic matter. Erosion by water and wind can remove fertile topsoil; practices like contour plowing and cover crops protect it."},{"t":"p","text":"Sustainability means meeting today's needs without harming future generations' ability to meet theirs."}]},{"say":"Water, wind and ice slowly reshape the land, carving canyons and building deltas.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Weathering breaks rock into smaller pieces. Erosion moves those pieces. Deposition drops them in a new place."},{"t":"list","items":["Mechanical weathering physically breaks rock (ice wedging, roots, abrasion).","Chemical weathering changes rock's minerals (acid rain dissolving limestone, rusting).","Agents of erosion: water (the most powerful), wind, glaciers and gravity (landslides)."]},{"t":"p","text":"Landforms created by these processes include canyons (like the Grand Canyon, carved by the Colorado River), deltas at river mouths, sand dunes, and U-shaped valleys carved by glaciers."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Stream table erosion","aim":"Run water through a stream table at different speeds and see the largest sediment it can move.","sets":[{"name":"v","start":50,"label":"Water speed (cm/s)","min":5,"max":300,"step":5}],"outs":[{"name":"d","expr":"(v/40)^2","label":"Largest grain moved (mm)"}],"steps":["Record the largest grain moved at 5 speeds.","Classify: clay < 0.004 mm, sand 0.06–2 mm, pebbles 4–64 mm."],"noise":5,"result":"Faster water carries much larger particles (competence rises roughly with the square of speed). When a river slows, it drops the biggest grains first, which is why deltas and floodplains sort sediment by size.","plot":{"x":"v","y":"d"}}]}]];
export default text;
